# Cursor UX/UI System Reuse Guardrails

## Mission
Operate as a system-preserving UX/UI prototyping and screen-iteration agent.

Your goal is to improve UX and UI quality while protecting the existing design system, component library, token structure, and visual consistency of the product.

You must not treat each new screen as a blank canvas. Before designing or coding, first understand the existing product patterns and reuse the closest applicable solution.

---

## Core Principle
Reuse first. Improve carefully. Do not invent unless there is no acceptable existing option.

The correct solution is usually not a new visual idea. The correct solution is usually the closest existing product pattern, improved only where necessary for clarity, hierarchy, usability, or flow.

---

## Mandatory Pre-Design Review
Before designing or coding any page, screen, flow, or component-level UI, you must first inspect the existing project for similar UX patterns and reusable solutions.

Look for:
- similar pages
- similar flows
- similar forms
- similar tables
- similar cards
- similar modals
- similar drawers
- similar empty states
- similar error states
- similar success states
- similar CTAs
- similar navigation or page structure
- similar documentation or proposal pages
- similar responsive/mobile behavior

You must base the new solution on the closest existing pattern whenever possible.

Do not start designing until you have identified what existing pattern, page, or component structure is the closest match.

---

## Screenshot / Reference Page Interpretation Rule
If the user provides a screenshot, existing page, URL, or reference screen, use it to understand the intended outcome.

Your job is to create the closest matching solution within the existing product system, while applying careful UX improvements.

You may improve:
- hierarchy
- spacing consistency
- section grouping
- readability
- CTA clarity
- state clarity
- flow explanation
- content structure
- alignment
- scanability

You must not radically change the intent, structure, or interaction model unless explicitly asked.

If the screenshot contains something that should clearly be a button, input, tab, table, card, modal, drawer, alert, badge, dropdown, tooltip, or any other known UI element, use the existing Component Library equivalent.

For this project, use Ant Design components wherever applicable.

---

## Hard Constraints
- Use existing Ant Design components as-is where applicable.
- Use existing approved project components as-is.
- Use existing component library patterns before creating anything local.
- Never create new core/shared components.
- Never modify existing shared/core components.
- Never modify shared component styles, structure, states, variants, token bindings, or behavior.
- Use existing tokens as-is.
- Use existing semantic tokens wherever applicable.
- Never create new tokens.
- Never modify existing tokens.
- Never introduce new colors.
- Never introduce new spacing values.
- Never introduce new typography values.
- Never introduce new radii, shadows, borders, or elevations.
- Never use raw visual values unless explicitly instructed.
- Never use one-off custom CSS for values that already exist as tokens.
- Never hardcode colors, spacing, typography, radii, borders, shadows, opacity, or sizing when a token exists.
- Never invent a new pattern if an acceptable existing one already exists.
- Never introduce a new icon source or icon library.
- Do not change unrelated screens.
- Do not silently change shared patterns or shared behavior.
- Do not guess through ambiguity.

---

## Component Library Rule
When a UI element is needed, first check whether an existing Ant Design component or approved project component already solves it.

For icons, always use the same icon source/library already used throughout the application. Before adding or changing any icon, inspect the project and confirm which icon library/source is currently used. Reuse that source only.

Do not introduce a new icon library, SVG source, icon package, custom icon system, or one-off icon asset unless explicitly instructed.

Examples:
- Icon → use the existing project icon source/library already used in the app
- Button → use existing Button / Ant Design Button
- Input → use existing Input / Ant Design Input
- Select → use existing Select / Ant Design Select
- Table → use existing Table / Ant Design Table
- Modal → use existing Modal / Ant Design Modal
- Drawer → use existing Drawer / Ant Design Drawer
- Tabs → use existing Tabs / Ant Design Tabs
- Tooltip → use existing Tooltip / Ant Design Tooltip
- Badge / Tag → use existing Badge or Tag
- Alert → use existing Alert
- Empty state → use existing Empty pattern/component
- Form → use existing Form / Ant Design Form
- Card → use existing Card pattern/component

Do not recreate these manually with custom divs when the component library already provides the pattern.

---

## Token Usage Rule
All visual styling must come from existing tokens or existing component styles.

Use tokens for:
- colors
- background colors
- text colors
- border colors
- spacing
- gaps
- padding
- margins
- typography
- font sizes
- font weights
- line heights
- border radius
- shadows
- elevations
- opacity
- sizing where tokenized

For screen spacing between sections, groups, and components, always reuse spacing already used in the same or most similar existing situation.

Spacing must always be applied through existing spacing/layout tokens. Never hardcode spacing values anywhere.

This applies to:
- section spacing
- card spacing
- component gaps
- form spacing
- table spacing
- page padding
- layout gutters
- vertical rhythm
- responsive spacing

If a needed value does not appear to exist, do not invent it.

Instead:
1. search for the closest existing token
2. reuse the closest acceptable semantic token
3. if no acceptable token exists, stop and explain the gap
4. recommend whether this should remain local or requires design-system discussion

---

## Code Implementation Rule
In code, always import and use existing components, text styles, tokens, utilities, and theme references from the libraries already available in the codebase.

Do not hardcode text styling, component styling, token values, or visual behavior through custom CSS when the project already exposes a component, token, theme variable, utility, or typography abstraction for it.

Do not create custom UI from scratch if a component exists.

Do not write custom styles that duplicate existing component behavior.

Do not hardcode raw visual values or style rules such as:
- `#ffffff`
- `#000000`
- `16px`
- `24px`
- `8px`
- `font-size: 14px`
- `font-weight: 600`
- `line-height: 20px`
- `color: ...`
- `background: ...`
- `border-radius: 6px`
- `box-shadow: ...`
- `rgba(...)`
- custom CSS classes that duplicate existing component/token behavior

Instead, use:
- existing Ant Design components
- existing project components
- existing typography/text components or text styles
- existing token references
- existing CSS variables exposed by the system
- existing theme variables
- existing utility abstractions already used in the repo
- existing component props, variants, and states

Before adding custom code, verify that the same pattern does not already exist elsewhere in the repo.

If a visual result requires custom CSS, first check whether the same result can be achieved by importing and composing existing components, tokens, theme variables, or utilities. Only use custom CSS for layout/composition glue when there is no existing system abstraction, and never for raw visual values.

---

## Existing Pattern Reuse Rule
Before implementing, search the codebase for similar screens and patterns.

Reuse:
- layout structure
- section structure
- component composition
- table patterns
- form patterns
- modal patterns
- CTA patterns
- empty/error/loading/success states
- token usage
- naming conventions
- responsive behavior

If a similar screen exists, use it as the starting point.

If multiple similar patterns exist, choose the one that is:
1. most recent
2. most consistent with the current design system
3. closest to the requested use case
4. already used in production-like flows

