## Figma-to-Code Parity Verification Protocol

Whenever a screen or component must be 1:1 with a Figma design — or after any substantive UI change — run this protocol in full before closing the task.

### Step 1 — Read the token source files

Before comparing anything, read the actual token definition files to know the real pixel value behind each token name:

- `src/theme/hydraTypography.ts` — all font sizes, line heights, font weights (base, small, large, heading 1–5, XL)
- `src/theme/hydraAntdTypography.ts` — how those map into antd token names (`fontSizeSM`, `fontSizeLG`, `fontSizeHeading4`, etc.)
- `src/theme/hydraAlias.ts` / `src/theme/antdTheme.ts` — spacing tokens (`paddingXS`, `paddingSM`, `paddingMD`, `paddingLG`, `marginXXS`, `marginXS`, `marginSM`, `marginMD`, `marginLG`, `marginXL`) and their px values

Build a mental (or inline comment) reference table, for example:

| Token | px |
|---|---|
| `token.fontSizeSM` | 12 |
| `token.fontSize` | 14 |
| `token.fontSizeLG` | 16 |
| `token.fontSizeXL` / `fontSizeHeading4` | 20 |
| `token.fontSizeHeading5` | 16 |
| `token.marginXXS` | 4 |
| `token.marginXS` | 8 |
| `token.marginSM` | 12 |
| `token.paddingSM` | 12 |
| `token.paddingMD` | 20 |
| `token.paddingLG` | 24 |
| `token.marginLG` | 24 |

Never assume a token's px value from memory. Always verify against the source file.

### Step 2 — Get exact Figma measurements

Use the Figma MCP (`get_design_context` with `nodeId` from the Figma URL) to retrieve the CSS output for each node. The output contains exact Tailwind-style classes, for example:

- `px-[24px]` → `paddingInline: 24px`
- `gap-[4px]` → gap: 4px
- `text-[length:var(--typography/font-size/fontsizeheading4,20px)]` → 20px heading 4
- `border-b border-[#f0f0f0]` → bottom border using `colorBorderSecondary`

If Figma MCP is unavailable, use the most recently captured design context from the existing documentation or screenshots, and document any assumption explicitly.

### Step 3 — Build a per-element comparison table

For every element that carries spacing, font size, or color — create a comparison:

| Element | Figma value | Token equivalent | Code (current) | Match? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Topbar padding | `px-24 py-12` | `paddingInline: paddingLG, paddingBlock: paddingSM` | `padding: paddingSM` | ✗ |
| Greeting title | `fontSizeHeading4 = 20px` | `Typography.Title level={4}` | `level={5}` | ✗ |
| Section gap | `gap-[24px]` | `gap={token.marginLG}` | `gap={token.marginSM}` | ✗ |
| Label font | `text-[14px]` | `token.fontSize` (no override) | `fontSizeSM` | ✗ |

Do not skip any property. Spacing, font size, font weight, color, border, and gap must each be checked.

### Step 4 — Fix systematically, one element at a time

For every mismatch found in Step 3:

1. Identify the correct token from the table built in Step 1.
2. Replace the incorrect value in code with the correct token reference.
3. Never introduce raw px or hex values to match Figma — always use the token.
4. If the correct Figma value has no matching token, stop and document the gap; do not invent a new value.

### Step 5 — Verify the component choice against Figma

Beyond spacing and font sizes, also confirm that the correct Ant Design component is used for each element:

- Figma shows an `Alert` node → use `<Alert>`, not a styled `<Card>`.
- Figma shows a `Select` → use `<Select>`, not a custom div.
- Figma shows a `Table` → use `<Table>`, not a manually built list.

The Figma design context labels nodes by component type. Match the component, not just the visual appearance.

### Step 6 — Run TypeScript check and linter

After all fixes:

```bash
npx tsc --noEmit
```

And run `ReadLints` on the edited file. Zero errors required before closing the task.

### When to run this protocol

Run the full protocol whenever:
- the user says "check parity", "1:1 check", or "compare with Figma"
- a screen is created or significantly changed
- spacing, font sizes, or component choices are questioned
- after any pass where you have made visual changes without a prior Figma comparison

Do not skip steps. A partial check is not a parity check.

---

## Visual Consistency Check
After generating or updating a screen, visually inspect the result against existing product patterns.

Check for:
- inconsistent spacing
- raw colors
- inconsistent typography
- wrong button hierarchy
- custom-looking components
- mismatched card styling
- inconsistent borders
- inconsistent shadows
- inconsistent radius
- alignment issues
- inconsistent empty/error/success states
- over-customized layouts
- visual drift from Ant Design/project patterns

If something visually deviates, fix it by reusing existing tokens/components/patterns, not by inventing new values.

---

## UX Improvement Rule
You may improve UX, but improvements must be conservative and system-compatible.

Allowed UX improvements:
- clearer hierarchy
- better grouping
- clearer section labels
- better CTA order
- improved progressive disclosure
- clearer state messaging
- better empty/error/success feedback
- reduced duplication
- better scanability
- better alignment with existing product flows

Not allowed unless explicitly requested:
- completely new interaction models
- new navigation systems
- new visual language
- new design system concepts
- new shared components
- large structural changes outside the requested screen

---

## CTA Rule
Keep one clear primary CTA per context.

Secondary actions must not visually compete with the primary action.

Destructive actions must never appear primary unless explicitly intended.

If multiple CTAs exist, define:
- primary action
- secondary action
- tertiary/helper action
- destructive action, if any

Use existing button variants from the component library.

---

## State Rule
Always consider relevant states:
- default
- hover, if applicable
- active, if applicable
- focus, if applicable
- loading
- empty
- error
- success
- disabled
- read-only
- no results
- missing data
- permission denied, if relevant
- unsaved changes, if relevant

Use existing project/Ant Design state patterns.

Do not create new state styling manually.

---

## Scope Rule
Only change the requested screen, page, or flow.

If the request impacts shared behavior, shared components, global tokens, navigation, layout shell, or other screens, stop and ask first.

Never modify shared/core system files unless explicitly instructed.

---

## Allowed Creative Exception
If no acceptable existing pattern exists, you may create a new local screen-level composition by combining existing atoms, Ant Design components, or approved existing project components.

This is allowed only if:
- existing shared/core components remain unchanged
- existing tokens remain unchanged
- the solution stays local to the requested screen/flow
- the solution is clearly treated as a local prototype
- the solution is not promoted as a new shared system component
- the implementation still uses existing tokens and approved components

Document this clearly in the response.

---

## Ambiguity / Uncertainty Rule
If requirements, component choice, token usage, spacing, layout behavior, or pattern selection are unclear, do not guess and do not implement.

Ask for clarification before implementation.

If useful, present 2–3 grounded options based on existing patterns, recommend one, and wait for direction.

Each option should explain:
- which existing pattern it uses
- which components it reuses
- what UX tradeoff it makes
- whether it is local or has broader impact

---

## Output Rule
When generating or iterating, briefly state:
- which existing pattern/page/component was reused
- which existing components were used
- which existing tokens were used or preserved
- whether any acceptable existing pattern was not found
- whether the solution is local or has broader product impact
- whether shared/core components remain unchanged
- whether shared tokens remain unchanged
- whether any raw values were avoided or found

Keep this report short, but always include it.

---

## Review Checklist Before Finalizing
Before finalizing any implementation, verify:

- [ ] Existing similar patterns were checked first
- [ ] Existing Ant Design/project components were used
- [ ] Existing app icon library/source was identified and reused
- [ ] No new icon library, SVG source, or custom icon system was introduced
- [ ] No new core/shared components were created
- [ ] No shared/core components were modified
- [ ] Existing tokens were used
- [ ] Existing spacing/layout tokens were used for all section and component spacing
- [ ] Spacing was reused from the same or most similar existing pattern
- [ ] Text styling was pulled from existing typography/text styles or components
- [ ] Component styling was pulled from existing components, props, variants, tokens, or theme variables
- [ ] No new tokens were created
- [ ] No raw visual values were introduced
- [ ] No hardcoded spacing values were introduced
- [ ] No unrelated screens were changed
- [ ] CTA hierarchy is clear
- [ ] Relevant states were considered
- [ ] The result visually matches the existing product system
- [ ] Any local-only composition is clearly documented

---

## Enforcement
Prefer reuse over invention.

Prefer consistency over novelty.

Prefer local safe iteration over broad system change.

Protect the design system first.

If the requested solution requires breaking these rules, stop and explain the conflict before proceeding.
